    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a 12-week hypertrophy block for a client, but they've completely stalled on their back squat for the last three sessions. No strength gains, no hypertrophy progress, just hitting a wall. Can you explain the physiological mechanics behind why plateaus happen and how implementing a deload week or adjusting volume might actually help them break through it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A 12-week hypertrophy block is a very serious undertaking, and it is absolutely normal to encounter a plateau, especially when the back squat is involved. The back squat is a massive compound movement that requires significant coordination and energy, so it is not surprising that the client has hit a wall.\n\nFrom a physiological standpoint, plateaus occur because the body adapts to the stress placed upon it. When the client has been squatting for three sessions without progress, their muscles and nervous system have likely become efficient at handling that specific load, meaning the stimulus is no longer sufficient to trigger further growth or strength gains. This is a natural biological response to consistent overload.\n\nTo break through this, implementing a deload week is an excellent strategy. A deload week allows the central nervous system to recover from accumulated fatigue and reduces systemic inflammation, which can be building up from the high volume of squats. By reducing the intensity or volume, the body can reset its adaptation mechanisms, making the subsequent training sessions feel fresher and more potent.\n\nAlternatively, adjusting the volume—perhaps by increasing the number of sets while keeping the weight the same, or vice versa—can provide a new stimulus that the body has not yet adapted to. This change",
      "source": "target",
